course: dictionary
lesson: 14013
title: Madly and its meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word madly comes from mad, but it does not always mean crazy. It is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens. «The dog ran madly around the garden, barking at the wind.» Madly tells you how the dog ran: in a wild, out-of-control way.
explain: Madly can also make another word stronger. «Mia was madly busy before the party.» Try the swap: if you put very busy in its place and the sentence still makes sense, madly means very much here.
explain: Feeling words often work this way too. «Tom is madly in love with his new puppy» does not mean Tom is crazy. It means he loves the puppy very much. Read the words around madly to decide which meaning fits.
fact: Madly is an adverb, so it often tells you how an action is done.
fact: «Madly» can mean in a wild, uncontrolled or frantic way, as in «rush about madly».
fact: «Madly» can also mean very much or extremely, as in «madly in love» or «madly jealous».
fact: In old-fashioned writing, «madly» can mean in a crazy or foolish way.
fact: Useful phrases: «rush about madly», «wave madly», «madly busy».
exercise: «The children rushed madly across the playground.» What does madly mean here? | *In a wild, frantic way | Without any shoes | Very quietly | Slowly and carefully
exercise: «Sam was madly busy all week before the school play.» What does madly mean here? | *Very much, extremely busy | In a wild way, shouting | Not busy at all | Sleepily
exercise: Which word tells you how Lily waved? «Lily waved madly at her friend across the road.» | Lily | *madly | friend | road
test: «The puppy chased its tail madly.» What does madly mean here? | Very quietly | *In a wild, out-of-control way | In a sleepy way | In a kind way
test: Ravi says madly always means crazy. Is he right? | *No, it can also mean very much, as in madly busy | Yes, it always means crazy | Yes, it always means happy | No, it means slowly
exam: «The crowd cheered madly when the team scored.» What does madly suggest about the crowd? | Quiet and bored | Sleepy and slow | *Wild and very excited | Polite and calm
